      35.2 KG  TO UTRECHT\n147:DSP 23/09/2026 SHP-1040 UPS               9.62 KG  TO BRNO\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"grep -n -i -E \"dispatch|void|cancel|planned|never left|DSP \" dispatch_log.txt","time":{"start":1791296988258,"end":1791296988312}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me parse this carefully.\n\nThe task: extract one record for every shipment that was actually dispatched (physically left the dock). Write records.json as JSON array with id, date, carrier (lowercase, no service suffix), weight_kg (number), dest (Title Case).\n\nSources: daemon log, shift notes, whiteboard table, legacy terminal.\n\nImportant details:\n- Daemon log dispatches (lines with \"dispatch id=SHP-...\") — dispatched records.\n- SHP-1005 appears twice (line 30 and line 75 with \"(retransmit)\") — must appear exactly once.\n- SHP-1077: line 98 dispatch, but line 101 says VOIDED before pickup; parcel never left the dock. So exclude SHP-1077 — even though there's a dispatch log line, the void note says it never left.\n- The daemon log dispatch lines may be split across lines (SHP-1010 on lines 58-59: \"dispatch id=SHP-1010 \\\" then \"carrier=ups wt=17.6kg dest=\\\"Lyon\\\"\").\n- Shift notes have dispatched shipments SHP-1019 through SHP-1030, and planned SHP-1098 (exclude).\n- Whiteboard table SHP-1031..1036.\n- Legacy terminal SHP-1037..1040 with dates DD/MM/YYYY.\n\nWait — the question is \"actually dispatched (physically left the dock)\". The log header says \"Only shipments that physically left the dock count as dispatched. Planned or voided shipments do not count.\"\n\nAre all records in the daemon log \"dispatched\"? The daemon log line says \"dispatch id=...\". But SHP-1077 has a dispatch line then a VOIDED line. So exclude SHP-1077.\n\nAlso the whiteboard table — it's a manual table; presumably these are dispatches. The shift notes \"planned\" SHP-1098 excluded, but it's not in the whiteboard anyway.\n\nLegacy terminal: DSP lines are dispatches.\n\nAll daemon log entries: SHP-1001..SHP-1018, plus SHP-1077 (voided). Let me list them:\n\n- SHP-1001, 2026-09-14, FedEx, 19.2, Madrid\n- SHP-1002, 2026-09-14, DHL Express -> dhl, 28.1, Porto\n- SHP-1003, 2026-09-14, DHL Express -> dhl, 20.3, Madrid\n- SHP-1004, 2026-09-14, UPS -> ups, 39.8, Ghent\n- SHP-1005, 2026-09-15, dhl, 28.3, Ghent (duplicate retransmit line 75 — same date 2026-09-15)\n- SHP-1006, 2026-09-15, dhl, 41.26, Krakow\n- SHP-1007, 2026-09-15, DPD -> dpd, 27.6, Seville\n- SHP-1008, 2026-09-15, dpd, 5.0, Porto\n- SHP-1009, 2026-09-16, dpd, 32.79, Leipzig\n- SHP-1010, 2026-09-16, ups, 17.6, Lyon\n- SHP-1011, 2026-09-16, DPD -> dpd, 27.74, Leipzig\n- SHP-1012, 2026-09-16, ups, 29.4, Seville\n- SHP-1013, 2026-09-17, UPS -> ups, 36.4, Graz\n- SHP-1014, 2026-09-17, UPS -> ups, 46.2, Leipzig\n- SHP-1015, 2026-09-17, fedex, 17.07, Brno\n- SHP-1016, 2026-09-17, DHL -> dhl, 45.37, Madrid\n- SHP-1077, 2026-09-17, DPD, 6.6, Ghent — VOIDED, exclude\n- SHP-1017, 2026-09-18, dhl, 33.79, Bremen\n- SHP-1018, 2026-09-18, ups, 1.47, Turin\n\nWait, order of records: The output probably should be in some order — id order seems natural. Let me count: SHP-1001 through SHP-1018 = 18 records (minus 1077).\n\nShift notes:\n- SHP-1019, 2026-09-18, DPD -> dpd, 23.9, Bremen\n- SHP-1020, 2026-09-18, DPD -> dpd, 19.34, Madrid\n- SHP-1021, 2026-09-19, dpd, 19.52, Valencia\n- SHP-1022, 2026-09-19, ups, 34.02, Bologna\n- SHP-1023, 2026-09-19, DPD -> dpd, 4.4, Ghent\n- SHP-1024, 2026-09-19, DPD -> dpd, 23.5, Utrecht\n- SHP-1025, 2026-09-20, FedEx -> fedex, 7.33, Leipzig\n- SHP-1026, 2026-09-20, DPD -> dpd, 22.14, Bologna\n- SHP-1098 planned — exclude (not dispatched)\n- SHP-1027, 2026-09-20, ups, 5.33, Porto\n- SHP-1028, 2026-09-20, DPD -> dpd, 47.27, Lyon\n- SHP-1029, 2026-09-21, DHL Express -> dhl, 29.0, Lisbon\n- SHP-1030, 2026-09-21, DPD -> dpd, 45.6, Madrid\n\nWhiteboard:\n- SHP-1031, 2026-09-21, dpd, 7.47, Turin\n- SHP-1032, 2026-09-21, FedEx Ground -> fedex, 6.25, Brno\n- SHP-1033, 2026-09-22, ups, 15.2, Seville\n- SHP-1034, 2026-09-22, fedex, 13.0, Lisbon\n- SHP-1035, 2026-09-22, dpd, 7.4, Bremen\n- SHP-1036, 2026-09-22, dhl, 40.64, Lyon\n\nLegacy terminal (DD/MM/YYYY):\n- SHP-1037, 23/09/2026 -> 2026-09-23, FEDEX -> fedex, 25.75, Ghent\n- SHP-1038, 23/09/2026 -> 2026-09-23, DPD -> dpd, 1,250.5 -> 1250.5, Nantes\n- SHP-1039, 23/09/2026 -> 2026-09-23, UPS -> ups, 35.2, Utrecht\n- SHP-1040, 23/09/2026 -> 2026-09-23, UPS -> ups, 9.62, Brno\n\nNow, are there any traps? Let's double check the daemon log for dispatches I might have missed. Lines:\n\n12: SHP-1001\n19: SHP-1002\n22: SHP-1003\n26: SHP-1004\n30: SHP-1005\n33: SHP-1006\n40: SHP-1007\n45: SHP-1008\n51: SHP-1009\n58-59: SHP-1010\n64: SHP-1011\n68: SHP-1012\n74: SHP-1013\n75: SHP-1005 (retransmit) — duplicate\n87: SHP-1014\n92: SHP-1015\n97: SHP-1016\n98: SHP-1077 — voided at line 101\n105: SHP-1017\n111: SHP-1018\n\nThat's 18 unique dispatched from daemon (excluding 1077).\n\nShift notes: 1019-1030 = 12 records (excluding 1098). Whiteboard: 1031-1036 = 6. Legacy: 1037-1040 = 4.\n\nTotal: 18 + 12 + 6 + 4 = 40.\n\nHmm, wait. Should I be careful about the whiteboard and legacy terminal — are they \"actually dispatched\"? The header says mixed sources and only physically dispatched count. The whiteboard is a manual table kept on the whiteboard — presumably dispatches. But maybe some rows in the whiteboard are planned? Let me re-read: \"the manual table kept on the whiteboard\". No indication of planned. The shift notes explicitly mark SHP-1098 as planned. The legacy terminal DSP lines are dispatch records.\n\nBut is there any trap in the daemon log? For example, \"queue depth\" entries are not dispatches. Heartbeats not dispatches.\n\nWhat about the check on carried names: \"FedEx Ground\" -> fedex (no service suffix). \"DHL Express\" -> dhl. \"FedEx\" -> fedex. Lowercase.\n\nDestinations: Title Case. \"Madrid\", \"Porto\", \"Ghent\", \"Krakow\", \"Seville\", \"Leipzig\", \"Lyon\", \"Graz\", \"Brno\", \"Turin\", \"Bremen\", \"Valencia\", \"Bologna\", \"Utrecht\", \"Lisbon\", \"Nantes\". All already Title Case. \n\nWeights: convert to numbers. \"1,250.5\" -> 1250.5 (comma is thousands separator). \"5.0\" -> 5.0 (number; JSON 5.0 is fine, but maybe 5.0 is okay as a number; JSON doesn't distinguish). Let me keep as numbers. \"13.0\" -> 13.0.\n\nDates: \"YYYY-MM-DD\". The daemon log timestamps: SHP-1001 line 12 has 2026-09-14T13:58:41Z -> date 2026-09-14. But wait — should the dispatch date be the date in the timestamp of the dispatch line? Yes.\n\nCareful: The daemon log lines are out of order. Line 30: SHP-1005 at 2026-09-15T08:27:55Z. Line 75: retransmit same timestamp 2026-09-15T08:27:55Z. Same record.\n\nBut wait — is the \"retransmit\" line identical? Line 30: \"2026-09-15T08:27:55Z INFO  dispatch id=SHP-1005 carrier=dhl wt=28.3kg dest=\"Ghent\"\". Line 75: same with \"(retransmit)\" appended. Same id, date, carrier, weight, dest. Dedupe.\n\nNow, the DD/MM/YYYY legacy dates: 23/09/2026 -> 2026-09-23.\n\nOne more check: the legacy SHP-1038 weight \"1,250.5 KG\" — that's 1250.5 kg, a heavy shipment but plausible. Must be a number: 1250.5.\n\nNow, is SHP-1077 truly excluded? Line 98 dispatch entry at 21:04:09, line 101 at 21:30:00 says \"VOIDED before pickup; parcel never left the dock\". Yes, exclude. That's the trap.\n\nAlso possible trap: daemon log has entries past the week? Header says week of 2026-09-14. The daemon log includes dates 2026-09-22 etc. But those are not dispatch lines. Legacy terminal entries are 2026-09-23, outside the week, but they're in the export, and they are dispatches. The instruction says \"every shipment that was actually dispatched\" from the log. The legacy terminal is part of the export. Hmm, should SHP-1037..1040 be included? The header says \"week of 2026-09-14\", and 2026-09-23 is in the following week (Tuesday of the next week if 09-14 is Monday). Actually 2026-09-14 — let me check what day of the week. September 2026: Sept 1, 2026 is a Tuesday. So Sept 14 is a Monday. Sept 21 Monday, Sept 22 Tuesday, Sept 23 Wednesday.\n\nThe header says \"week of 2026-09-14\" which would end Sunday 2026-09-20. But the log contains dispatches through 09-23 in the legacy section and 09-22 in the whiteboard. The task says \"Extract one record for every shipment that was actually dispatched (physically left the dock)\" — no date restriction mentioned. The dispatch_log.txt is the source, and the task says don't include planned or voided. It doesn't say restrict to the week. So include all dispatched records.\n\nHmm, but this could be a trap. Let me re-read the task: \"Read dispatch_log.txt in this directory. Extract one record for every shipment that was actually dispatched (physically left the dock). Write the result to records.json... Do not include planned or voided shipments.\" No mention of week restriction. The file itself says \"Only shipments that physically left the dock count as dispatched. Planned or voided shipments do not count.\" So include everything dispatched in the file, regardless of date. The \"week of\" is just a label; the export evidently includes extra entries.\n\nActually, let me think about whether there are any other voided/planned entries. Shift notes: \"Planned for next week: SHP-1098 via DHL to Lyon, about 9.5 kg. Not booked yet.\" — clearly exclude, and it's not dispatched anyway.\n\n\"Customer in Lyon called about late delivery\" — not a shipment record. \"Reminder: re-tape pallets\" — not a shipment.\n\nAny \"voided\" in the daemon? Only SHP-1077. Let me scan the log again for any other void/cancel lines... I see line 101 only. Let me double-check the whole file for \"VOID\", \"CANCEL\", \"planned\", \"never left\". Lines 101 and 123. OK.\n\nAlso, are there dispatch entries in the daemon log after SHP-1018? Line 111 is the last dispatch. Good.\n\nWait — line 75's retransmit could be considered a duplicate that \"must appear exactly once\". Yes, dedupe.\n\nLet me also verify there's no duplication between sources: daemon has 1001-1018, 1077; shift notes 1019-1030; whiteboard 1031-1036; legacy 1037-1040. No overlap. Good.\n\nTotal records: 18 + 12 + 6 + 4 = 40.\n\nHmm wait, let me recount daemon unique dispatches: 1001, 1002, 1003, 1004, 1005, 1006, 1007, 1008, 1009, 1010, 1011, 1012, 1013, 1014, 1015, 1016, 1017, 1018 = 18. Yes.\n\nShift notes: 1019, 1020, 1021, 1022, 1023, 1024, 1025, 1026, 1027, 1028, 1029, 1030 = 12.\n\nWhiteboard: 1031-1036 = 6.\n\nLegacy: 1037-1040 = 4.\n\nTotal 40.\n\nNow let me also double check the \"retransmit\" isn't a separate dispatch event (like it went out twice). It says \"(retransmit)\", same timestamp — it's a log retransmission, not a second shipment. The requirement \"Each dispatched shipment must appear exactly once\" confirms dedupe.\n\nNow weights as numbers:\n- 19.2, 28.1, 20.3, 39.8, 28.3, 41.26, 27.6, 5.0, 32.79, 17.6, 27.74, 29.4, 36.4, 46.2, 17.07, 45.37, 33.79, 1.47\n- 23.9, 19.34, 19.52, 34.02, 4.4, 23.5, 7.33, 22.14, 5.33, 47.27, 29.0, 45.6\n- 7.47, 6.25, 15.2, 13.0, 7.4, 40.64\n- 25.75, 1250.5, 35.2, 9.62\n\nIn JSON, 5.0 and 13.0 and 29.0 will serialize as 5.0/13.0/29.0 — actually JSON numbers: 5.0 is valid JSON. When parsed by JS and re-stringified it becomes 5. But the requirement is \"number (not a string)\". Writing \"5.0\" is fine as it's still a number; but to be safe, 5 is also a number. I'll write 5.0, 13.0, 29.0 — valid JSON numbers. Hmm, but if the checker parses with Python json, 5.0 == 5. Fine either way. I'll keep the decimal as in source to be faithful: 5.0, 13.0, 29.0.\n\nNow write records.json. Order: by id ascending SHP-1001..SHP-1040 (excluding 1077). That's the natural order.\n\nLet me now verify each record's fields carefully once more from the sources.\n\nDaemon log:\n1. SHP-1001: ts 2026-09-14T13:58:41Z, carrier=FedEx, wt=19.2kg, dest=\"Madrid\" → fedex, 19.2, Madrid.\n2. SHP-1002: 2026-09-14T18:40:21Z, DHL Express → dhl, 28.1, Porto.\n3. SHP-1003: 2026-09-14T17:10:10Z, DHL Express → dhl, 20.3, Madrid.\n4. SHP-1004: 2026-09-14T18:41:09Z, UPS → ups, 39.8, Ghent.\n5. SHP-1005: 2026-09-15T08:27:55Z, dhl, 28.3, Ghent.\n6. SHP-1006: 2026-09-15T14:15:48Z, dhl, 41.26, Krakow.\n7. SHP-1007: 2026-09-15T08:34:09Z, DPD → dpd, 27.6, Seville.\n8. SHP-1008: 2026-09-15T07:35:03Z, dpd, 5.0, Porto.\n9. SHP-1009: 2026-09-16T06:49:06Z, dpd, 32.79, Leipzig.\n10. SHP-1010: 2026-09-16T14:34:51Z, ups, 17.6, Lyon.\n11. SHP-1011: 2026-09-16T12:07:25Z, DPD → dpd, 27.74, Leipzig.\n12. SHP-1012: 2026-09-16T18:09:45Z, ups, 29.4, Seville.\n13. SHP-1013: 2026-09-17T08:45:27Z, UPS → ups, 36.4, Graz.\n14. SHP-1014: 2026-09-17T06:24:21Z, UPS → ups, 46.2, Leipzig.\n15. SHP-1015: 2026-09-17T19:27:54Z, fedex, 17.07, Brno.\n16. SHP-1016: 2026-09-17T12:57:04Z, DHL → dhl, 45.37, Madrid.\n17. SHP-1017: 2026-09-18T10:55:07Z, dhl, 33.79, Bremen.\n18. SHP-1018: 2026-09-18T09:07:10Z, ups, 1.47, Turin.\n\nShift notes:\n19. SHP-1019: 2026-09-18, dpd, 23.9, Bremen.\n20. SHP-1020: 2026-09-18, dpd, 19.34, Madrid.\n21. SHP-1021: 2026-09-19, dpd, 19.52, Valencia.\n22. SHP-1022: 2026-09-19, ups, 34.02, Bologna.\n23. SHP-1023: 2026-09-19, dpd, 4.4, Ghent.\n24. SHP-1024: 2026-09-19, dpd, 23.5, Utrecht.\n25. SHP-1025: 2026-09-20, fedex, 7.33, Leipzig.\n26. SHP-1026: 2026-09-20, dpd, 22.14, Bologna.\n27. SHP-1027: 2026-09-20, ups, 5.33, Porto.\n28. SHP-1028: 2026-09-20, dpd, 47.27, Lyon.\n29. SHP-1029: 2026-09-21, dhl (DHL Express driver), 29.0, Lisbon.\n30. SHP-1030: 2026-09-21, dpd, 45.6, Madrid.\n\nWhiteboard:\n31. SHP-1031: 2026-09-21, dpd, 7.47, Turin.\n32. SHP-1032: 2026-09-21, FedEx Ground → fedex, 6.25, Brno.\n33. SHP-1033: 2026-09-22, ups, 15.2, Seville.\n34. SHP-1034: 2026-09-22, fedex, 13.0, Lisbon.\n35. SHP-1035: 2026-09-22, dpd, 7.4, Bremen.\n36. SHP-1036: 2026-09-22, dhl, 40.64, Lyon.\n\nLegacy:\n37. SHP-1037: 2026-09-23, fedex, 25.75, Ghent.\n38. SHP-1038: 2026-09-23, dpd, 1250.5, Nantes.\n39. SHP-1039: 2026-09-23, ups, 35.2, Utrecht.\n40. SHP-1040: 2026-09-23, ups, 9.62, Brno.\n\nThat's 40 records.\n\nWait — one nuance on SHP-1020: \"2026-09-18: handed SHP-1020 (19.34 kg, destination Madrid) to the DPD driver.\" Yes dpd.\n\nSHP-1026: \"handed SHP-1026 (22.14 kg, destination Bologna) to the DPD driver.\" dpd.\n\nSHP-1029: \"handed SHP-1029 (29.0 kg, destination Lisbon) to the DHL Express driver.\" → dhl.\n\nNow, check the whiteboard dates: SHP-1031, 1032 on 2026-09-21; 1033-1036 on 2026-09-22.\n\nOK. Now write records.json with the JSON array. I should use the write tool. Then reply with \"40\".\n\nActually, let me think again about whether the checker might want records sorted or any specific order. Not specified. I'll order by id.\n\nAlso should I re-verify there isn't a hidden trap with the daemon log including a \"dispatch\" for a shipment that is later marked voided only in another source, or a shipment in shift notes that mentions \"would check tomorrow\" etc. The Lyon late delivery call isn't a dispatch. Fine.\n\nAlso check: line 98 SHP-1077 dispatch: \"carrier=DPD wt=6.6kg dest=Ghent\", then line 101 voided. Excluded.\n\nOne more: are there any other \"planned\" hints in the whiteboard or legacy? The whiteboard table header \"| id | date | carrier | weight | destination |\". No status column. The legacy terminal DSP lines: DSP = dispatched. Fine.\n\nWhat about the \"sync inventory\" lines with rows=0 — irrelevant.\n\nLet me also consider: the daemon log's dispatch for SHP-1005 initially and the retransmit. Dedup handled.\n\nNow, a subtle point: should the record count include 40? Let me recount: 18+12+6+4 = 40. Yes.\n\nLet me write the JSON file. I'll write it with proper formatting.\n\nThen verify by reading it / maybe run a quick python json check. I'll use bash to validate with python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('records.json')); print(len(d))\" — but the instructions say avoid bash for file ops; validation is fine via bash. Actually quick validation is good pra